1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,820
What if the next customer workshop started with a reusable studio, instead of a blank page?

2
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:14,449
We use AI to help create the learning experience around a customer scenario: hands-on labs, an
editable delivery deck, and explainer videos.

3
00:00:14,449 --> 00:00:16,089
The goal is simple.

4
00:00:16,089 --> 00:00:21,418
Less repetitive preparation, more time for customer understanding and great facilitation.

5
00:00:21,418 --> 00:00:23,903
The architect still owns the outcome.

6
00:00:25,033 --> 00:00:29,388
In a fast-moving AI space, a workshop is never really finished.

7
00:00:29,388 --> 00:00:33,512
Features evolve, audiences differ, and customer constraints change.

8
00:00:33,512 --> 00:00:36,787
Rebuilding each artifact independently creates drift.

9
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:42,046
A lab says one thing, a slide says another, and the instructor has to bridge the gap.

10
00:00:42,046 --> 00:00:50,641
Our approach turns that treadmill into a learning loop: create a coherent package, validate it,
deliver it, and feed the lessons into the next version.

11
00:00:51,742 --> 00:00:55,521
Start with the scenario, the audience, and an observable outcome.

12
00:00:55,521 --> 00:00:58,306
AI reasoning helps shape the learning journey.

13
00:00:58,306 --> 00:01:02,060
Code generation helps turn ideas into runnable exercises.

14
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:06,825
Visual composition and speech tools help explain the same concepts in different ways.

15
00:01:06,825 --> 00:01:11,954
Bounded agents coordinate the work, while reusable skills make the process consistent.

16
00:01:11,954 --> 00:01:16,434
These are complementary capabilities, not a magic one-click workshop.

17
00:01:16,434 --> 00:01:19,363
Tool availability and human review still matter.

18
00:01:20,492 --> 00:01:26,876
For hands-on labs, AI helps translate learning goals into a sequence of small, achievable tasks.

19
00:01:26,876 --> 00:01:32,581
Each task needs clear steps, an observable checkpoint, and a short recap of why it matters.

20
00:01:32,581 --> 00:01:38,200
We can generate starter material, solution paths, troubleshooting guidance, and catch-up points.

21
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,340
Then we run the exercises.

22
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,624
The standard is not that the instructions sound convincing.

23
00:01:43,624 --> 00:01:47,129
It is that a learner can follow them and reach the intended outcome.

24
00:01:48,242 --> 00:01:53,101
For the delivery deck, AI helps us move from slide surgery to story design.

25
00:01:53,101 --> 00:01:58,066
We can create a new narrative, or reshape an existing workshop for a different customer.

26
00:01:58,066 --> 00:02:04,415
That includes adjusting depth, replacing examples, tightening the flow, and adding presenter
cues.

27
00:02:04,415 --> 00:02:06,255
The deck stays editable.

28
00:02:06,255 --> 00:02:14,484
The important part is alignment: the presentation introduces what the labs actually teach, with
the same terminology, sequence, and outcomes.

29
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:19,281
Explainer videos extend the workshop beyond the live session.

30
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:24,861
Students can preview a concept, replay a tricky step, or return to a checkpoint later.

31
00:02:24,861 --> 00:02:33,075
Future instructors need a different explanation: pacing, demo cues, common pitfalls, and where to
cut or rescue an exercise.

32
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:36,765
AI helps draft and narrate those audience-specific stories.

33
00:02:36,765 --> 00:02:40,459
Captions and transcripts make the material easier to navigate.

34
00:02:40,459 --> 00:02:44,359
When the source changes, the affected media needs to change with it.

35
00:02:45,492 --> 00:02:49,001
We formalized the approach as a suite of agents and skills.

36
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:51,501
The builder owns the end-to-end package.

37
00:02:51,501 --> 00:02:53,650
A researcher gathers evidence.

38
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:56,430
A producer creates bounded deliverables.

39
00:02:56,430 --> 00:02:58,749
A reviewer challenges the result.

40
00:02:58,749 --> 00:03:04,549
Skills capture the reusable playbooks for resources, design, labs, media, and verification.

41
00:03:04,549 --> 00:03:06,828
Agents answer who owns the work.

42
00:03:06,828 --> 00:03:09,413
Skills answer how good work gets done.

43
00:03:09,413 --> 00:03:14,767
A new scenario changes the brief and examples, while the production discipline stays reusable.

44
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:17,597
A new domain still needs subject-matter review.

45
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:21,850
Speed only helps if the workshop is trustworthy.

46
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:29,464
We check technical claims, execute the labs, inspect the rendered slides, and reconcile narration
with the final visuals.

47
00:03:29,464 --> 00:03:32,914
We protect customer information and use approved tools.

48
00:03:32,914 --> 00:03:39,108
Automated checks catch some problems, but they do not replace an instructor rehearsal or subject-
matter review.

49
00:03:39,108 --> 00:03:41,438
Human approval is the release gate.

50
00:03:41,438 --> 00:03:46,412
The loop is generate, verify, rehearse, and improve, not generate and hope.

51
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,553
For the Customer Success Unit, this creates three opportunities.

52
00:03:51,553 --> 00:03:57,127
First, faster workshop creation because we are not starting every artifact from scratch.

53
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:06,022
Second, easier reuse and sharing across Cloud Solution Architects, because the package includes
editable sources, guidance, and a repeatable structure.

54
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:11,011
Third, less repetitive work when tailoring an existing workshop or creating a new one.

55
00:04:11,011 --> 00:04:15,956
Measure the value through preparation effort, learner progress, and reuse across teams.

56
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:21,340
For customers, a connected learning experience is easier to follow.

57
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:25,269
The story, the exercise, and the checkpoint reinforce one another.

58
00:04:25,269 --> 00:04:29,098
Clear structure reduces the effort of figuring out what to do next.

59
00:04:29,098 --> 00:04:37,008
Useful leave-behinds include the editable deck, labs, explainers, and transcripts, so teams can
revisit the material after the event.

60
00:04:37,008 --> 00:04:43,808
Customization starts with their goals, skill level, and constraints, rather than asking them to
fit a generic demonstration.

61
00:04:44,908 --> 00:04:52,578
Imagine adapting the same approach for a knowledge-discovery session, an operations scenario, or
a customer-specific AI prototype.

62
00:04:52,578 --> 00:04:57,092
We change the audience, examples, environment, and success criteria.

63
00:04:57,092 --> 00:05:03,677
We keep the useful structure: objectives, runnable labs, checkpoints, presenter guidance, and
review gates.

64
00:05:03,677 --> 00:05:07,366
Then we revalidate what changed and regenerate the affected media.

65
00:05:07,366 --> 00:05:09,761
Reuse is not copy and forget.

66
00:05:09,761 --> 00:05:13,560
It is a controlled starting point that gets better with delivery feedback.

67
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:18,104
The point is not to produce more content for its own sake.

68
00:05:18,104 --> 00:05:20,899
It is to scale the craft of a great workshop.

69
00:05:20,899 --> 00:05:24,688
Start with one customer scenario and one clear learning outcome.

70
00:05:24,688 --> 00:05:30,048
Build a connected package, keep the sources reusable, and release it only after review.

71
00:05:30,048 --> 00:05:35,118
Then measure preparation effort, learner progress, and what the next instructor can reuse.

72
00:05:35,118 --> 00:05:39,452
Let AI handle more of the busywork, so we can focus on the customer.
